---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Figure from '~/components/Figure.astro';
import Assumptions from '~/components/Assumptions.astro';
import Callout from '~/components/Callout.astro';
import Math from '~/components/Math.astro';
import Badge from '~/components/Badge.astro';
import Cite from '~/components/Cite.astro';
import BodyOrderLab from '~/islands/BodyOrderLab';

const assumptions = [
  { term: 'Potential', value: 'Stillinger–Weber silicon with its published parameters as distributed in LAMMPS (Si.sw): ε = 2.1683 eV, σ = 2.0951 Å, a = 1.8, λ = 21, γ = 1.2, cos θ₀ = −1/3, A = 7.0496, B = 0.6022, p = 4, q = 0.' },
  { term: 'Geometry', value: 'Three atoms in a plane, the centre atom at the origin; open boundaries, no other neighbours.' },
  { term: 'Check', value: 'The same code gives diamond silicon at a = 5.431 Å exactly −2ε = −4.3366 eV per atom (Atlas test suite).' },
];

const counts = [2, 4, 6, 12].map((n) => ({
  n,
  pairs: n,
  triplets: (n * (n - 1)) / 2,
  quads: (n * (n - 1) * (n - 2)) / 6,
}));
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Pairs, angles and body order"
  description="Physics Lab P3: why a pairs-only energy cannot express bond angles, how a three-body term with an infinitely smooth cutoff fixes it (Stillinger–Weber silicon), and how modern MLIPs reach high body order."
>
  <article class="page-grid lab-page">
    <header class="wide head">
      <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/physics-lab/">Physics Lab</a> · P3</p>
      <h1 class="h-display">Pairs, angles and body order</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Silicon is tetrahedral because its bonds prefer 109.5° to each other. An energy made of pairs alone cannot know
        that. This experiment uses a real classical potential to show what each extra “body” in an energy term buys.
      </p>
      <div class="head-badges">
        <Badge label="live-calculation" />
        <Badge label="theory" />
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="block" id="angles" aria-labelledby="a-h">
      <h2 id="a-h" class="h-section">Two bodies, then three</h2>
      <p>The Stillinger–Weber potential <Cite id="stillinger1985sw" /> writes the energy of silicon as pairs plus triplets centred on each atom:</p>
      <Math display number="1" tex={String.raw`E = \sum_{i<j}\varphi_2(r_{ij}) + \sum_i\sum_{j\neq i}\sum_{k>j}\lambda\varepsilon\,\big(\cos\theta_{jik} + \tfrac13\big)^2\, e^{\gamma\sigma/(r_{ij}-a\sigma)}\,e^{\gamma\sigma/(r_{ik}-a\sigma)}`} />
      <p>
        The three-body term is zero exactly at cos θ = −⅓, the tetrahedral angle, and grows on either side. Without it,
        three atoms with fixed bond lengths have the same energy at any angle wide enough to put the outer pair beyond
        the cutoff (above about 107° here), and the lowest energy at narrow angles, where the outer pair adds a bond: a
        pairs-only model is blind to bond angles and drifts towards close packing.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="wide fig-wrap">
      <Figure title="Three silicon atoms: pairs only versus pairs plus angles" labels={['live-calculation', 'theory']} number={1} id="fig-body" framed={false}>
        <BodyOrderLab client:visible />
        <Fragment slot="caption">
          Left: the triplet with its bonds (teal intensity = pair energy), the angle arc at the centre atom (amber =
          angle strain), a green tick at 109.47° and the cutoff sphere. Right: the triplet energy against the angle for
          both models, and the radial pair energy with the three-body screening, both reaching zero smoothly at aσ.
        </Fragment>
        <Assumptions slot="assumptions" items={assumptions} />
        <Fragment slot="source">Potential: <Cite id="stillinger1985sw" />; parameters and functional form as distributed with LAMMPS (potentials/Si.sw, pair_style sw).</Fragment>
      </Figure>
    </div>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="c-h">
      <h2 id="c-h" class="h-section">An infinitely smooth cutoff</h2>
      <p>
        Both terms are switched off by factors of the form exp(σ/(<i>r</i> − <i>a</i>σ)). As <i>r</i> approaches
        <i>a</i>σ from below, this factor and every one of its derivatives go to zero, so the energy is infinitely
        differentiable at the cutoff — smoother than the cosine cutoff (continuous slope) or the polynomial envelope
        (continuous curvature) in the <a href="/method/design-system/#fig-cutoff">cutoff explorer</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="b-h">
      <h2 id="b-h" class="h-section">Body order in machine-learned potentials</h2>
      <p>
        Counting terms shows why high body order is expensive when written out explicitly. For an atom with <i>N</i>
        neighbours inside the cutoff:
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="tx">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Neighbours N</th><th>Pairs (2-body)</th><th>Triplets (3-body)</th><th>Quadruplets (4-body)</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {counts.map((c) => (
              <tr><td>{c.n}</td><td>{c.pairs}</td><td>{c.triplets}</td><td>{c.quads}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The atomic cluster expansion avoids the combinatorial sum: it first projects the neighbourhood onto a basis, one
        sum over neighbours, and then takes products of those projections, which reach any body order at a cost linear in
        <i>N</i> <Cite id="drautz2019ace" />. MACE uses the same idea inside equivariant message passing, combining the
        products with the tensor products of <a href="/physics-lab/harmonics/">P5</a> <Cite id="batatia2022mace" />.
      </p>
      <Callout kind="note" title="Not a fitting exercise">
        Stillinger–Weber is used here because its parameters are published and its behaviour is easy to check. The point
        is structural: what an energy can express depends on the bodies its terms see, before any data is fitted.
      </Callout>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lab-page {
    padding-block: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4rem) clamp(3rem, 6vw, 5rem);
  }
  .head .eyebrow a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .head h1 {
    margin-block: 0.6rem 1rem;
    font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 4.4vw, 3.6rem);
    line-height: 1.05;
  }
  .lede {
    font: 1.1875rem/1.6 var(--font-serif);
    color: var(--atlas-ink-muted);
    max-width: 44rem;
  }
  .head-badges {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.35rem;
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }
  .block {
    margin-top: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    font: 1.0625rem/1.7 var(--font-serif);
    color: var(--atlas-ink);
  }
  .block p {
    margin-block: 0 1rem;
  }
  .block a {
    color: var(--atlas-arch);
  }
  .fig-wrap {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
  .table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }
  .tx {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font: 0.9rem/1.45 var(--font-sans);
    min-width: 28rem;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .tx th,
  .tx td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.45rem 1.25rem 0.45rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--atlas-rule);
  }
  .tx th {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--atlas-ink-muted);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.03em;
  }
</style>
